Title: Standing guard: a skill name hardcoded in a script survives the skill's rename
Created: 2026-09-16
Author: Integration Test

--- log ---
2026-09-16T08:58Z Integration Test created
2026-09-16T09:02Z Integration Test note Filed from /roar step 4 after PR #925; the class fired once and the sweep found 9 names across 5 scripts

--- body ---
## Context

PR #925 renamed the skill `memory` to `memory-sweep`. The sweep was keyed on
two search terms — the path `skills/memory` and the command `/memory` — and
both are structurally blind to a third form: the skill name as a **bare
identifier inside a script**. Two live regressions got through and were caught
only by an external review, not by `make check`:

- `scripts/census/memory-recall.py` — `re.compile(rb'"skill":\s*"(dream|roar|lair|memory)"')`
- `scripts/trace-stats.py` — `MANDATED_SKILLS = {"roar", "lair", "dream", "molt", "memory", ...}`

Neither had test coverage of the classifier, so the suite stayed green while
the scripts silently stopped recognising the renamed skill. Both were fixed in
#925 and both now have tests.

The class is wider than the instance. A `/roar` step-3 sweep of `origin/main`
found **9 skill names hardcoded across 5 scripts**:

| Skill | Files |
|---|---|
| `dream`, `gaze`, `molt`, `verify-gate` | `scripts/trace-stats.py` |
| `lair`, `roar` | `scripts/census/memory-recall.py`, `scripts/trace-stats.py` |
| `merge` | `scripts/guard-cd-primary-repo.sh`, `scripts/guard-worktree-identity.sh` |
| `zotero-import` | `scripts/zotero-import.py` |

Renaming any of them repeats the defect, silently.

## The design question this ticket owns

The obvious guard — "every skill name hardcoded in a script must match an
existing `skills/` directory" — **misfires on the correct fix**. #925
deliberately kept `"memory"` beside `"memory-sweep"`, because these scripts
read a historical trace corpus spanning both sides of the rename. A naive
guard flags that as a dangling name. So the guard needs a way to express
"this name is intentionally historical", and that is the part worth designing
rather than improvising.

Do not auto-write the test before settling this. `rules/workflow.md` warns
against growing a guard whose defect class misfires, and a guard that cries
wolf on every deliberate historical alias is worse than none.

## Relevant files

- `scripts/trace-stats.py` — `MANDATED_SKILLS`, `BUILTIN_COMMANDS`
- `scripts/census/memory-recall.py` — `SKILL`, `CMD` regexes
- `scripts/guard-cd-primary-repo.sh`, `scripts/guard-worktree-identity.sh` — `merge`
- `scripts/zotero-import.py` — `zotero-import`
- `tests/test_memory_recall.py`, `tests/test_trace_stats.py` — the per-instance
  tests #925 added; the standing guard is the class-level complement, not a
  replacement
- `rules/authoring-skills.md` — carries the naming rules, including the
  reserved-name rule #925 added

## Actions

1. Decide how a deliberately-historical name is declared. Candidates: an
   inline marker comment the guard parses, a single declared alias map, or
   scoping the guard to names that no script comment marks.
2. Write the guard as an `adherence`-tier test.
3. Re-run the step-3 sweep to confirm the 9 known names either resolve or are
   explicitly declared historical.

## Test

Red step: a test that fails on a fixture script naming a skill directory that
does not exist, and passes when the same name carries the historical marker
chosen in action 1.

## Verification

- [ ] The guard fails on a deliberately dangling skill name
- [ ] The guard passes on `"memory"` in `MANDATED_SKILLS`, which is correct
- [ ] `make check` green

## Invariants

- The two per-instance tests from #925 keep passing unchanged
- No script's runtime behaviour changes; this ticket adds a check, not a fix

## Exit criteria

Renaming any skill in this repo produces a red gate rather than a silent
classifier regression, without flagging the deliberate historical aliases
that #925 established.
